'''trivoltin''' (trī-vol′tin), ''n.'' [< L. ''tres'' (''tri-''), three, + It. ''volto'', turn: see ''volt''<sup>1</sup>.] A race of the silkworm of commerce (''Sericaria mori'') which has three annual generations, thus producing three crops of cocoons each year; also, such a silkworm. Also ''trivoltine''.

'''triweekly''' (trī-wēk′li), ''a.'' [< ''tri-'' + ''weekly''.] 1. Occurring, performed, or appearing once every three weeks.—2. Less correctly, occurring, performed, or appearing thrice a week: as, a ''triweekly'' newspaper.

'''Trixagidæ''' (trik-saj′i-dē), ''n. pl.'' A family of beetles: same as ''Throscidæ''.

'''Trixagus''' (trik′sa-gus), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. τριξός, var. of τρισσός, τριττός, threefold (< τρεῖς (τρι-), three), + ἄγειν, drive, do.] A genus of beetles: same as ''Throscus''.

'''trizomal''' (trī-zō′mal), ''a.'' [For *''trirhizomal'', < Gr. τρεῖς (τρι-), three, + ῥίζωμα, root, + ''-al''.] Formed of the sum of three square roots.—'''Trizomal curve,''' {{smaller|a curve whose equation is}}

{{block center|<math>\sqrt{\alpha X} + \sqrt{\beta Y} + \sqrt{\gamma Z} = 0,</math>}}

{{smaller|where α, β, γ are parameters, and X, Y, Z three curves of the same system.}}

